nks the money you earn to the costs used to earn it. Cash basis is simpler and shows real cash flow, which can be easier for very small businesses. In short: accrual shows performance; cash shows cash movement. If you want, I can look up official guidance (IRS or accounting standards) and add citations. I didn’t use web sources for this plain-language explanation.","runId":"run_95c8702cc05f17eb","sampleIndex":2,"schemaVersion":"2.0.0","status":"completed","strategy":"compiler_slice","structuralRetention":{"allArtifactCriticalPoliciesRetained":true,"criticalPolicyIds":["universal_honesty","no_hidden_reasoning_reveal","no_background_work_claims","do_not_mention_internal_policy","do_not_invent_facts","no_raw_tool_json_exposure","privacy_floor","authoritative_sources_preferred"],"selectedPolicyIds":["universal_honesty","no_hidden_reasoning_reveal","no_background_work_claims","do_not_mention_internal_policy","do_not_invent_facts","no_raw_tool_json_exposure","privacy_floor","citations_required","authoritative_sources_preferred","citation_definition"]},"toolCalls":[],"toolCostUsd":0.0,"totalTokens":6152,"trialId":"trial_4a30604264287e7c"}
